1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,949
It's Friday. Payday. You and your coworkers

2
00:00:02,849 --> 00:00:04,964
all just got paid the same amount.

3
00:00:05,164 --> 00:00:06,507
Four thousand dollars.

4
00:00:06,857 --> 00:00:08,790
Jake is already talking about the new

5
00:00:08,690 --> 00:00:09,930
truck he just financed.

6
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:12,143
Mia is planning a weekend trip she'll

7
00:00:12,043 --> 00:00:13,229
put on a credit card.

8
00:00:13,579 --> 00:00:15,697
And you? You already know exactly where

9
00:00:15,597 --> 00:00:16,835
every dollar is going.

10
00:00:17,035 --> 00:00:19,486
Because you made that decision once, months ago.

11
00:00:19,836 --> 00:00:20,913
Two weeks from now,

12
00:00:20,813 --> 00:00:22,713
Jake and Mia will be checking their

13
00:00:22,613 --> 00:00:24,616
balance, wondering where it all went.

14
00:00:24,816 --> 00:00:25,548
You won't.

15
00:00:25,898 --> 00:00:27,584
This isn't about earning more.

16
00:00:27,484 --> 00:00:29,012
It's about one simple rule.

17
00:00:29,212 --> 00:00:30,264
Here's how it works,

18
00:00:30,164 --> 00:00:31,692
and how you can start tonight.

19
00:00:32,042 --> 00:00:34,428
The rule is called fifty, thirty, twenty.

20
00:00:34,628 --> 00:00:36,579
Fifty percent of your take home pay

21
00:00:36,479 --> 00:00:37,319
goes to needs.

22
00:00:37,669 --> 00:00:39,214
Thirty percent goes to wants.

23
00:00:39,414 --> 00:00:41,713
And twenty percent goes to your future.

24
00:00:41,613 --> 00:00:43,348
Savings, and paying off debt.

25
00:00:43,698 --> 00:00:45,497
On four thousand dollars a month,

26
00:00:45,397 --> 00:00:47,041
that's two thousand for needs,

27
00:00:46,941 --> 00:00:48,328
twelve hundred for wants,

28
00:00:48,228 --> 00:00:50,079
and eight hundred for your future.

29
00:00:50,429 --> 00:00:52,910
Simple, right? But the magic isn't the math.

30
00:00:53,110 --> 00:00:55,156
The magic is that you decide before

31
00:00:55,056 --> 00:00:56,212
the money shows up.

32
00:00:56,562 --> 00:00:58,143
Let's start with needs.

33
00:00:58,043 --> 00:01:00,589
Rent. Groceries. Utilities. Insurance.

34
00:01:00,489 --> 00:01:01,618
Getting to work.

35
00:01:01,818 --> 00:01:03,438
The things you'd still pay for if

36
00:01:03,338 --> 00:01:04,082
life got hard.

37
00:01:04,432 --> 00:01:06,175
Here's where most people get stuck.

38
00:01:06,375 --> 00:01:08,548
Jake's truck payment is eight hundred and

39
00:01:08,448 --> 00:01:09,711
ninety dollars a month.

40
00:01:10,061 --> 00:01:11,651
Add his rent and insurance,

41
00:01:11,551 --> 00:01:13,859
and his needs are already eating seventy

42
00:01:13,759 --> 00:01:15,184
percent of his paycheck.

43
00:01:15,384 --> 00:01:17,022
There's nothing left to save,

44
00:01:16,922 --> 00:01:18,559
before the month even starts.

45
00:01:18,909 --> 00:01:20,392
You drive a ten year old car

46
00:01:20,292 --> 00:01:21,182
that's paid off.

47
00:01:21,382 --> 00:01:23,346
It's not exciting. But it costs you

48
00:01:23,246 --> 00:01:24,784
almost nothing every month.

49
00:01:25,134 --> 00:01:27,879
If your needs are over fifty percent, don't panic.

50
00:01:28,079 --> 00:01:29,810
Look at the big three first.

51
00:01:29,710 --> 00:01:31,149
Housing, car, and food.

52
00:01:31,049 --> 00:01:32,955
That's where the real money is.

53
00:01:33,305 --> 00:01:35,003
Now the fun part. Wants.

54
00:01:35,203 --> 00:01:37,039
Eating out. Streaming. Hobbies.

55
00:01:36,939 --> 00:01:40,062
That concert. Yes, you're allowed to enjoy your money.

56
00:01:40,412 --> 00:01:42,449
This is what most budgets get wrong.

57
00:01:42,349 --> 00:01:43,956
They try to cut all the fun.

58
00:01:44,156 --> 00:01:46,145
And then people quit after two weeks.

59
00:01:46,495 --> 00:01:48,052
With fifty, thirty, twenty,

60
00:01:47,952 --> 00:01:49,077
the fun is planned.

61
00:01:49,277 --> 00:01:50,463
So when you spend it,

62
00:01:50,363 --> 00:01:51,962
you spend it with zero guilt.

63
00:01:52,312 --> 00:01:54,167
Mia doesn't have a wants budget.

64
00:01:54,067 --> 00:01:55,373
She has a credit card.

65
00:01:55,573 --> 00:01:58,038
And it charges her over twenty percent interest.

66
00:01:58,388 --> 00:02:00,279
That weekend trip will cost her a

67
00:02:00,179 --> 00:02:01,636
lot more than she thinks,

68
00:02:01,536 --> 00:02:03,264
if it takes months to pay off.

69
00:02:03,614 --> 00:02:05,523
And now the part that changes your

70
00:02:05,423 --> 00:02:07,492
life. Twenty percent for your future.

71
00:02:07,692 --> 00:02:09,887
For you, that's eight hundred dollars a month.

72
00:02:10,237 --> 00:02:12,119
The trick? It leaves your account the

73
00:02:12,019 --> 00:02:13,178
same day you get paid.

74
00:02:13,379 --> 00:02:15,435
Automatically. You never even see it.

75
00:02:15,785 --> 00:02:18,057
First, you build an emergency fund.

76
00:02:17,957 --> 00:02:19,609
A few months of expenses,

77
00:02:19,509 --> 00:02:21,347
sitting there, just in case.

78
00:02:21,697 --> 00:02:23,610
Then, if you have high interest debt,

79
00:02:23,510 --> 00:02:24,247
you crush it.

80
00:02:24,446 --> 00:02:26,608
And after that, you start investing for

81
00:02:26,508 --> 00:02:27,295
the long run.

82
00:02:27,645 --> 00:02:29,038
Fast forward one year.

83
00:02:29,238 --> 00:02:30,614
The office is buzzing.

84
00:02:30,514 --> 00:02:31,832
Rumors about layoffs.

85
00:02:32,182 --> 00:02:34,333
Jake can't sleep. One missed paycheck,

86
00:02:34,233 --> 00:02:35,844
and the truck is in trouble.

87
00:02:36,044 --> 00:02:37,572
Mia's card is maxed out.

88
00:02:37,922 --> 00:02:40,499
You? You have almost ten thousand dollars saved.

89
00:02:40,699 --> 00:02:42,295
You're not happy about the news.

90
00:02:42,195 --> 00:02:43,651
But you're not scared either.

91
00:02:44,001 --> 00:02:46,252
That's what money really buys. Not stuff.

92
00:02:46,452 --> 00:02:48,149
Options. And peace of mind.

93
00:02:48,499 --> 00:02:50,083
Now jump five years ahead.

94
00:02:50,283 --> 00:02:52,206
If that eight hundred a month was

95
00:02:52,106 --> 00:02:54,416
invested and grew around seven percent a

96
00:02:54,316 --> 00:02:56,349
year, it could be worth about fifty

97
00:02:56,249 --> 00:02:57,620
seven thousand dollars.

98
00:02:57,970 --> 00:02:59,384
Markets go up and down,

99
00:02:59,284 --> 00:03:00,812
so nothing is guaranteed.

100
00:03:01,012 --> 00:03:03,434
But time and consistency are on your side.

101
00:03:03,784 --> 00:03:05,624
Jake is on his second truck loan.

102
00:03:05,824 --> 00:03:08,598
Mia is still paying for trips she barely remembers.

103
00:03:08,948 --> 00:03:10,343
And here's the crazy part.

104
00:03:10,243 --> 00:03:11,838
You all earned the same money.

105
00:03:12,038 --> 00:03:13,710
The only difference was a plan.

106
00:03:14,060 --> 00:03:15,295
So how do you start?

107
00:03:15,195 --> 00:03:17,623
Tonight, take your monthly take home pay.

108
00:03:17,523 --> 00:03:20,632
Not your salary. What actually lands in your account.

109
00:03:20,982 --> 00:03:22,557
Multiply it by point five,

110
00:03:22,457 --> 00:03:24,090
point three, and point two.

111
00:03:24,290 --> 00:03:25,705
Those are your three numbers.

112
00:03:26,055 --> 00:03:27,716
Then look at last month's spending.

113
00:03:27,916 --> 00:03:29,932
Be honest. Which bucket is too big?

114
00:03:30,282 --> 00:03:32,262
You don't have to be perfect on day one.

115
00:03:32,462 --> 00:03:34,752
Even sixty, thirty, ten is a huge

116
00:03:34,652 --> 00:03:36,876
win if you saved nothing before.

117
00:03:37,226 --> 00:03:40,388
Last step. Set up an automatic transfer for payday.

118
00:03:40,588 --> 00:03:42,600
That's it. You just became the person

119
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:43,427
who saves first.

120
00:03:43,777 --> 00:03:46,164
Next payday, your coworkers will be wondering

121
00:03:46,064 --> 00:03:47,333
where their money went.

122
00:03:47,533 --> 00:03:48,527
You'll already know.

123
00:03:48,877 --> 00:03:50,863
If this helped, subscribe for a new

124
00:03:50,763 --> 00:03:52,373
simple money move every day.

125
00:03:52,573 --> 00:03:53,969
And tell me in the comments.

126
00:03:53,869 --> 00:03:55,682
What's your biggest bucket right now?
